"""The Way Back Home, ages 6-9: 7 weeks. Memory verses are CSB (as in the site's 6-10 series, checked on bible.com)."""

WEEKS = [
dict(n=1, col='#1e9e5a', soft='#e3f5ea', title='God’s perfect home', pages=['The garden', 'The one rule'], book='pages 1–2',
  bible='Genesis 1:1, 26–31; 2:8–9, 15–17', photo='garden-end', thumbs=['garden-clip', 'garden-end', 'rule-end'],
  heart=('Before there was a problem, there was a home. God made a good world, made people to be with Him, and gave them everything to enjoy except one tree. '
         'Six- to nine-year-olds think in concrete terms, so let Eden be real and wonderful: rivers, fruit, animals, and God Himself with them. '
         'The one rule was not God holding something back; it was a loving Father giving His children a real way to trust Him. The rest of the series only makes sense if they first see how good home was.'),
  big='God made it good, and He made me to be with Him!',
  moves='Sweep both arms wide on “good”, point up on “Him”, then hug yourself.',
  verse=('God saw all that he had made, and it was very good indeed.', 'Genesis 1:31'),
  skill=('Find the first book', 'Genesis is the very first book of the Bible. Show them the contents page. Explain a reference: Genesis (book) 1 (big number = chapter) : 31 (small number = verse). Everyone finds Genesis 1:31; first finder reads it aloud.'),
  hook=('Mystery bag', 'Hide things God made in a bag: a leaf, a stone, a feather, an apple, a shell. Children reach in without looking and guess. “Who made all of these?” God made everything, and it was all good!'),
  hookneed=['Cloth bag with 5–6 things from nature'],
  drama=('Act it out: The one rule', 'Choose an Adam, an Eve, and three or four “trees” (children holding paper fruit). The teacher narrates as God: “You may eat from this tree… and this one… and this one…” Adam and Eve happily taste. Then at the last tree: “But not this one.” Freeze. Ask the audience: “How many yeses? How many noes?”'),
  pause=[('The garden', 'What made Eden perfect? (Not just the trees: God was with them.)'), ('The one rule', 'Why does Adam point at the tree? (So Eve would remember.)')],
  talk=[('What was the very best thing about Eden?', 'God was with them, and they loved being with Him.'),
        ('How many rules did God give Adam and Eve?', 'Just one! Every other tree was a “yes”.'),
        ('Your mom or dad says, “Don’t touch the hot stove.” Is that a mean rule or a loving rule?', 'Loving. God’s rule was too.')],
  game=('Move it or lose it', 'In small groups, children invent a motion for each part of the verse, then perform it for the class. Say it three times with motions, then twice with motions only (lips closed!).'),
  act=('The one-rule game', ['Tape to mark one square on the floor'], 'Simon Says where everything is allowed except one thing: “Don’t step in the square!” Call out actions that lead close to it (hop, spin, tiptoe backwards). Afterwards count: how many yeses? One no. That was Eden.'),
  live='Every night this week, thank God for three good things He made.',
  pray='Go around: each child finishes the sentence, “Thank You, God, for making…” Then the teacher closes.',
  six='Six-year-olds may not read the verse yet. Pair each with a reader, and let them do the motions and say the reference.',
  nine='Ask nines: “If God made everything good, where did the bad come from?” Don’t answer it; tell them to come back next week.',
  tip='Keep this week bright and happy. The sadness comes next week and means more once they have enjoyed how good Eden was. Hand out the Road Home map today.',
  care=None, ask='What was the one rule God gave Adam and Eve? Was it a loving rule?',
  pages_kid=['Match the days of creation', 'Memory verse fill-in + draw', 'Comic strip: tell the story'], extra=[]),

dict(n=2, col='#7b3fe4', soft='#efe6fd', title='The big lie', pages=['The serpent', 'The fruit', 'Out of the garden'], book='pages 3–5',
  bible='Genesis 3:1–24', photo='fruit-end', thumbs=['serpent-end', 'fruit-end', 'exile-end'],
  heart=('The serpent’s first move was to question God’s word (“Did God really say…?”), then to flatly contradict it (“You won’t die!”). Eve and Adam believed the lie. '
         'Sin is not just a slip; it is choosing not to trust God. Everything changed: shame, fear, hiding, and the long walk out of the garden. '
         'Yet God came looking for them, clothed them (animals died to do it), and made the first promise of the gospel: Someone would crush the serpent (Genesis 3:15).'),
  big='Trust God, not the snake!', moves='Point up on “Trust God”, wiggle a hand like a snake on “not the snake”, and shake your head.',
  verse=('Every word of God is pure; he is a shield to those who take refuge in him.', 'Proverbs 30:5'),
  skill=('Open to the middle', 'Open a Bible right in the middle: you usually land in Psalms. Proverbs is the next book. Race to find Proverbs 30:5. Show the semicolon: it joins two halves of one thought.'),
  hook=('Two boxes', 'Show a beautifully wrapped box and a plain paper bag. Children vote which to open. The pretty box holds an old onion; the plain bag holds the treats. “The fruit looked good, but the snake’s promise was a trick.”'),
  hookneed=['Pretty gift box with an onion inside', 'Plain bag with a treat per child'],
  drama=('Act it out: The trick', 'A “serpent” (a child with a sock puppet) whispers lines you hand them on cards: “Did God really say that?” “You won’t die!” Eve and Adam act out looking, taking, eating, then hiding behind a chair. God (teacher) calls, “Where are you?” Freeze and ask: “Who told the truth?”'),
  pause=[('The serpent', 'What were the serpent’s two tricks? (Questioning God; then a lie.)'), ('The fruit', 'Stop on “Uh-oh.” What is sin? (Not trusting and obeying God.)'),
         ('Out of the garden', 'What did God promise? (Someone would come to crush the serpent and open the way home.)')],
  talk=[('What did the serpent say? Was it true?', 'No. He twisted God’s words: “Did God really say…?”'),
        ('Have you ever seen an ad that made a toy look amazing, but it wasn’t?', 'Things can look good and still be a trick.'),
        ('How did God make clothes for Adam and Eve?', 'Animals had to die. Remember this; it comes back in week 5!')],
  game=('Word-card scramble', 'Write the verse on index cards, one or two words each. Two teams race to put their set in order; the winners read it aloud together. Then remove two cards and race again.'),
  act=('Twisty snake', ['Kids’ page 3 (the snake spiral)', 'Scissors, crayons, string or yarn'], 'Children colour the snake, cut around the circle, then along the spiral line, and hang it from a string. As it twists, read the words on the head: “Trust God!”'),
  live='When something whispers “It’s fine, nobody will know,” stop and ask: “What does God say?”',
  pray='Ask God to help us trust what He says, even when something else looks good.',
  six='Sixes will love the onion. Keep the big idea to one line: “The snake lied. God always tells the truth.”',
  nine='Nines can spot the serpent’s strategy: question, lie, promise. Ask, “Where do we hear tricks like that today?”',
  tip='Call the serpent what the Bible calls him: a liar. End on hope: God made a promise that Someone would come.',
  care='The fruit page has a lightning flash and thunder. The book also says animals died to make the clothes. Say it plainly and briefly; it is the first picture of a substitute, and it matters in week 5.',
  ask='What did God promise Adam and Eve, even after they sinned?',
  pages_kid=['True or trick?', 'Secret-code memory verse', 'Twisty snake craft'], extra=[]),

dict(n=3, col='#3d3a8c', soft='#e7e6f6', title='Every one of us', pages=['The long road', 'God’s law'], book='pages 6–7',
  bible='Exodus 20:1–17; Romans 3:23; James 2:10', photo='test-end', thumbs=['road-end', 'test-clip', 'test-end'],
  heart=('Adam and Eve’s children had children, all the way down to us, and every one of them carried sin. God gave His law, the Ten Commandments, and like a mirror it shows what we could not see: '
         'we have all broken it. James 2:10 says breaking one law makes us guilty of all. This age group has a strong sense of fairness; they are quick to spot others’ sin and slow to see their own. '
         'Your honest “Me too” is the most important thing you will say this week.'),
  big='Everybody sins. Me too.', moves='Point around the whole room on “Everybody”, then point to yourself on “Me too.”',
  verse=('For all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God.', 'Romans 3:23'),
  skill=('Old and New Testament', 'The Bible has two big parts. The Old Testament (Genesis to Malachi) is before Jesus was born; the New Testament (Matthew to Revelation) is from His birth on. Find where the New Testament starts, then find Romans 3:23 (after the four Gospels and Acts).'),
  hook=('Mirror, mirror', 'Pass a hand mirror around. Each child says one thing they see (a smile, messy hair, a smudge). “A mirror shows what is really there. Today God’s law is a mirror for our hearts.”'),
  hookneed=['Hand mirror'],
  drama=('Act it out: The long road', 'Everyone lines up and walks slowly around the room as “all the people”, with a dot sticker on each hand (sin marks). At the front, two leaders hold up paper “stone tablets”. As each person reaches them, the leader reads one commandment and everyone stops: “Have you ever…?” Everyone looks at their dot.'),
  pause=[('The long road', 'What does every person on the road have? (Sin marks.)'), ('God’s law', 'Pause after the questions: answer quietly in your heart, not out loud.')],
  talk=[('What did every person on the long road have?', 'Sin marks, just like Adam and Eve.'),
        ('“That’s only two of the ten!” Why does breaking even one rule matter?', 'James 2:10: break one, and you’ve broken God’s law.'),
        ('What does God’s law do, like a mirror?', 'It shows our sin. (Next week: what it can’t do.)')],
  game=('Erase the verse', 'Write the verse on a whiteboard. Read it together, then a child erases one word. Read it again with the gap. Keep going until the board is empty and the class can say it all.'),
  act=('Fall-short toss', ['Bucket or basket', 'Paper balls, 3 per child', 'Tape for a throwing line'], 'Put the bucket far away, much too far. Everyone throws from the line; almost every ball falls short. Read the verse: sin means missing God’s perfect mark, and nobody can throw that far: not the teacher, not the pastor, not anyone.'),
  live='Notice one time this week when you do wrong. Tell God about it, right then.',
  pray='Quietly, in your heart, tell God you have broken His good rules. (No one says theirs out loud.)',
  six='Use concrete words: lying, taking, being mean, not obeying. Never use a child as the example; use yourself.',
  nine='Nines will want to argue it’s unfair. Let them: “Is one lie enough to make someone a liar?” Then read James 2:10.',
  tip='Never ask children to confess sins out loud. Throw too, and fall short yourself: “I miss the mark too.” Then it isn’t a lecture.',
  care='The book says “that makes you a liar and a thief.” That is the point of the law page, so don’t soften it, but say it about all of us (yourself first), never about one child.',
  ask='Which two of God’s rules did the book ask about? Has everybody broken them?',
  pages_kid=['Word search', 'Verse scramble', 'Story cards in order'], extra=['Small round dot stickers']),

dict(n=4, col='#e0453a', soft='#fde7e5', title='Locked out', pages=['Locked out', 'Can we scrub it off?'], book='pages 8–9',
  bible='Romans 3:20; Isaiah 59:2; Romans 6:23; Ephesians 2:8–9', photo='scrub-end', thumbs=['locked-end', 'scrub-clip', 'scrub-end'],
  heart=('A mirror shows the dirt but can’t wash it off; God’s law shows our sin but can’t remove it (Romans 3:20). Sin separates us from God (Isaiah 59:2), and the wages of sin is death (Romans 6:23). '
         'The children in the book scrub and scrub: being extra good, saying sorry, going to church. The marks stay. Children this age are natural rule-keepers and many already believe that being good is how you get to God. '
         'This week gently takes that hope away, so next week’s good news lands as a gift.'),
  big='I can’t scrub sin off!', moves='Scrub one arm hard with the other hand, then stop and shake your head on “can’t”.',
  verse=('For you are saved by grace through faith, and this is not from yourselves; it is God’s gift.', 'Ephesians 2:8'),
  skill=('The letters', 'After the Gospels and Acts come letters written to churches. Teach the trick for four of them in a row: “Go Eat Pop Corn” = Galatians, Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians. Find Ephesians 2:8.'),
  hook=('The locked box', 'Show a box with a padlock: there is a prize inside. Let children try: pulling, shaking, saying “please”, being extra good. Nothing works. “Sin locks us out of God’s home, and we don’t have the key.” Keep the box shut until next week!'),
  hookneed=['Box with a padlock (hide the key)'],
  drama=('Act it out: The scrub', 'Two volunteers play the children at the fountain. They try everything you call out: “Scrub harder!” “Be extra good!” (they sit up straight) “Say sorry!” “Go to church!” After each, the class checks their dot stickers from last week: still there. “Who can help?”'),
  pause=[('Locked out', 'What happened to the gates? (They slammed shut.) Why? (Sin locks us out.)'), ('Can we scrub it off?', 'Name three ways the children tried. Did any work?')],
  talk=[('What does a mirror do? What can’t it do?', 'It shows the dirt, but it can’t wash it off. God’s law is the same.'),
        ('Ever spilled juice on a white shirt? Did scrubbing get it all out?', 'Let them tell stories.'),
        ('Someone has to take our place. Who could it be?', 'Leave it as a question until next week!')],
  game=('Post-it cover-up', 'Write the verse on the board. Say it together, then cover one word with a sticky note. Repeat until every word is covered. Peel them off one at a time as a celebration.'),
  act=('The mark that won’t come off', ['White plastic plate (keep it for week 6!)', 'Permanent marker', 'Water, soap, sponge'], 'Draw a small “sin mark” on the plate. Children take turns scrubbing with water, soap and the sponge. Count the scrubs together. It stays! Put the plate away: “Next week, who has the key? In two weeks, who can make us clean?”'),
  live='When you think, “I need to be good enough for God,” remember: being saved is God’s gift.',
  pray='Tell God we can’t clean ourselves, and thank Him that the story isn’t over.',
  six='Sixes understand “locked out” and “won’t wash off”. Keep hell to what the book says, and move to hope.',
  nine='Ask nines: “If being good can’t fix it, what would?” Let them guess, and hold the answer until next week.',
  tip='Keep the padlock key for week 5 and the marked plate for week 6. Test rubbing alcohol on a marked plate at home first.',
  care=('The “Locked out” page says that if we die with our sin we will be punished in a place called hell, far away from God forever. Say it calmly, as the book does, then point ahead: '
        '“That is very bad news, but it is not the end of the story.” Watch for anxious children and sit with them after: “Jesus came to make a way. You’ll see next week.”'),
  ask='Why couldn’t the children scrub their marks off? What is God’s gift?',
  pages_kid=['Crossword', 'Gift-box verse', 'Comic strip: what are they thinking?'], extra=[]),

dict(n=5, col='#f07b1e', soft='#fdeedd', title='The door', pages=['The door', 'The cross'], book='pages 10–11',
  bible='John 10:9; John 3:16; John 14:6; Isaiah 53:6', photo='door-end', thumbs=['door-clip', 'door-end', 'clean'],
  heart=('Here is the good news! The Someone God promised in the garden is Jesus, God’s own Son. “I am the door. Whoever comes in through Me will be saved” (John 10:9). '
         'Jesus never sinned, yet He died in our place, like the animals that died for Adam and Eve. All our sin was put on Him (Isaiah 53:6). '
         'After four weeks of bad news, this is the turning point. Slow down and let it land: He did it because He loves us.'),
  big='Jesus is the door! He took my place.', moves='Swing both arms open like a door on “Jesus is the door”, then a hand on your heart on “He took my place.”',
  verse=('For God loved the world in this way: He gave his one and only Son, so that everyone who believes in him will not perish but have eternal life.', 'John 3:16'),
  skill=('The Gospels', 'The first four books of the New Testament tell Jesus’ story: Matthew, Mark, Luke, John (say it as a chant). Find John 3:16. Many people call it the most famous verse in the Bible.'),
  hook=('I’ll take your place', 'Say someone broke the class rules and must do 20 jumping jacks. Pick a child to come up. Just before they start: “Wait. I’ll do them for you.” Do all 20 yourself, puffing hard. “How did that feel?” That is what Jesus did, but He paid with His life.'),
  hookneed=['Last week’s locked box, with the key'],
  drama=('Act it out: Come and see!', 'Half the class are “the crowd”, milling around. Three “children” go to a doorway (two leaders holding hands up), look through, and run back shouting, “Come and see! There is a way back to God!” Each time one person follows them through, until the whole crowd is through.'),
  pause=[('The door', 'What are the people doing? (Telling others, “Come and see!”)'), ('The cross', 'Jesus never sinned. Why did He die? (In our place, because He loves us.)')],
  talk=[('Who is the Someone God promised back in the garden?', 'Jesus, God’s own Son.'),
        ('Jesus said, “I am the door.” What does a door do?', 'It lets you in! Now open last week’s locked box: Jesus is the key.'),
        ('Jesus never sinned. Why does that matter?', 'Only someone with no sin could take our place.')],
  game=('Discover the verse', 'Hand out paper plates, each with a chunk of John 3:16 (about 10 chunks). Without being told the verse, the children holding plates line up in order. Everyone else checks in their Bibles. Then say it together, plate by plate.'),
  act=('Door craft', ['Kids’ page 3', 'Scissors, glue sticks, crayons'], 'Colour the wall and door, cut the door on its thick lines, fold it open, and glue the top panel over the picture. Open the door and say: “Jesus is the door! He took my place.”'),
  live='Tell Jesus thank you, every day this week, for taking your place.',
  pray='Thank Jesus for dying in our place because He loves us.',
  six='Sixes: “Jesus died for me because He loves me.” Then promise more next week: the story isn’t over!',
  nine='Nines can connect the dots: the animals in Genesis 3, the “Someone” of the promise, the cross. Ask them to explain it back to you.',
  tip='Never end on the cross without a hint of Sunday: “But that’s not the end of the story.”',
  care='On the cross page one of the three children (Chase) turns away with his arms crossed. If they ask: “He doesn’t want Jesus yet. Jesus still loves him.” The kids’ pages use the picture of the children kneeling in white.',
  ask='Who is the door? What did Jesus do for us?',
  pages_kid=['Maze through the crowd', 'John 3:16 fill-in', 'Door that opens'], extra=[]),

dict(n=6, col='#e8a200', soft='#fdf3d6', title='He is alive!', pages=['The choice', 'The empty tomb'], book='pages 12–13',
  bible='Romans 10:9, 13; Luke 24:1–8; 1 Corinthians 15:3–4; Isaiah 1:18', photo='tomb-end', thumbs=['choice-clip', 'choice-end', 'tomb-end'],
  heart=('Three days after Jesus died, His friends found the tomb empty. He was alive, having beaten sin and death. Now everyone has a choice: some say “No thanks” and walk away; '
         'others say sorry to God, believe Jesus died in their place, and trust Him. God then takes their sin away and covers them with Jesus’ goodness, “as white as snow” (Isaiah 1:18). '
         'Explain the choice clearly this week; save the invitation for next week so no one feels rushed.'),
  big='Jesus is alive! He makes me clean!', moves='Jump up with arms high on “Jesus is alive!”, then wipe your hands clean on “He makes me clean!”',
  verse=('If you confess with your mouth, “Jesus is Lord,” and believe in your heart that God raised him from the dead, you will be saved.', 'Romans 10:9'),
  skill=('Luke and Romans', 'Find Luke 24:6, “He is not here, but he has risen!” Then find Romans 10:9. Romans comes right after Acts. Which one is a Gospel? Which one is a letter?'),
  hook=('The empty tomb box', 'A box with a paper-plate “stone” for a lid. “Jesus’ friends came to His tomb on Sunday morning…” Slowly roll the stone away. Empty! Shout together: “He is not here! He is alive!”'),
  hookneed=['Box with a paper-plate “stone”', 'The marked plate from week 4 + rubbing alcohol and a cloth'],
  drama=('Act it out: Sunday morning', 'Roles: two guards, three women carrying “spices”, an angel. Guards fall asleep; the women walk slowly, sadly; the angel says, “He is not here, but he has risen!” The women run back to tell the others. Swap roles and do it again.'),
  pause=[('The choice', 'What two choices do people make? (“No thanks”, or “I’m sorry, please save me!”)'), ('The empty tomb', 'Where is Jesus? What did He beat? (Sin and death.)')],
  talk=[('What two choices did people make in the book?', '“No thanks”, or “I’m sorry, please save me!”'),
        ('What does it mean to trust Jesus?', 'Turn from sin, and count on Jesus instead of on being good.'),
        ('How clean does God make us?', 'As white as snow (Isaiah 1:18).')],
  game=('Balloon juggle', 'One child keeps two balloons in the air while the class says the verse. Each round, cover a phrase on the board. If a balloon touches the floor before the verse ends, the next child tries.'),
  act=('Clean plate + sleeping soldiers', ['Plate from week 4', 'Rubbing alcohol or whiteboard cleaner (test first!)'], 'Bring back the plate: nothing worked last time. Wipe it with the cleaner: gone! “We couldn’t. Jesus can.” Then Sleeping soldiers: everyone lies still guarding the tomb. Anyone who moves helps catch movers. Shout “He is alive!” and everyone jumps up cheering.'),
  live='Tell one person this week: “Jesus is alive!”',
  pray='Praise Jesus that He is alive and makes us clean.',
  six='Sixes: the clean plate says it all. “We couldn’t. Jesus can.”',
  nine='Nines: walk through the verse’s two parts: what you say (mouth) and what you believe (heart). Ask what “Lord” means. (Boss, King: the One in charge.)',
  tip='Make clear the cleaner is only a picture: Jesus really takes sin away.',
  care='On the choice page Chase walks away (“No thanks”). That is the point of the page: everyone has a real choice. Keep your tone warm, and never ask children to choose publicly.',
  ask='What did Jesus’ friends find on the third day? What does Jesus do for people who trust Him?',
  pages_kid=['Secret code', 'Mouth and heart verse', 'Roll-away stone'], extra=['Paper fasteners (brads)']),

dict(n=7, col='#1e88e5', soft='#e2f0fc', title='Welcome home', pages=['Heaven’s gates', 'The new earth', 'Follow Jesus'], book='pages 14–16',
  bible='John 14:2–3; John 20:27; Revelation 21:1–5; Matthew 28:19–20', photo='gates-end', thumbs=['gates-clip', 'gates-end', 'new-earth-end'],
  heart=('The story ends where it began, at home with God. One day everyone who trusts Jesus will run through heaven’s open gates, and the same Jesus who sent Adam and Eve out will welcome His children home, '
         'the scars still in His hands. God will make everything new, even better than Eden. Until then, Jesus sends us to tell others. '
         'This week reviews the whole story, explains A-B-C, and invites each child, quietly and without pressure, to follow Jesus.'),
  big='Jesus welcomes me home, and I can bring a friend!', moves='Open your arms wide on “welcomes me home”, then wave a friend over on “bring a friend”.',
  verse=('Look, I am making everything new.', 'Revelation 21:5'),
  skill=('The last book', 'Revelation is the very last book. Genesis begins with a garden; Revelation ends with a new heaven and earth. Find Genesis 1:1 with one hand and Revelation 21:5 with the other: the whole story between your hands!'),
  hook=('Welcome-home tunnel', 'Hang streamers or balloons at the door. Leaders make a high-five tunnel and cheer each child in by name: “Welcome home!” Ask: how did that feel? It’s a tiny picture of heaven’s open gates.'),
  hookneed=['Streamers or balloons'],
  drama=('Act it out: Running home', 'Leaders stand at the far end as the open gates. One at a time, children run the length of the room as the narrator reads the gates page; a leader (as Jesus) opens their arms wide. Point to your hands: “the scars of His love.”'),
  pause=[('Heaven’s gates', 'Who is waiting? What is in His hands? (Scars: the scars of His love.)'), ('The new earth', 'What will be better than Eden?'), ('Follow Jesus', 'Read the A-B-C slowly. Pause here.')],
  talk=[('Who is waiting at heaven’s gates? What is in His hands?', 'Scars: the scars of His love.'),
        ('What will the new earth be like?', 'Even better than Eden!'),
        ('Who could you bring this week?', 'Fill in the invite card.')],
  game=('Story relay', 'Teams race to put the 12 story pictures (kids’ page 1) in order, then tell the whole story back, one picture each. Finish by saying all seven memory verses’ references in order.'),
  act=('Gates + invite card', ['Kids’ page 3', 'Scissors, glue sticks'], 'Make the gates that open to show Jesus welcoming the children home. Then cut out the invite card, write a friend’s name, and take it to them: the QR code plays the whole book.'),
  live='Give your invite card to a friend, and tell them one part of the story.',
  pray=('Read A-B-C together. Then: “I’m going to pray. If you want to follow Jesus, you can pray this quietly in your heart with me. You don’t have to.” '
        'Pray: “Dear God, I know I have sinned, and I am sorry. Thank You for sending Jesus to die for me and rise again. Please forgive me and make me clean. I want to follow You. In Jesus’ name, amen.” '
        'Then: “If you prayed that, or have questions, come and tell me after.”'),
  six='Sixes copy each other. Keep the invitation quiet and personal; never count or announce responses.',
  nine='Nines may ask real questions (“How do I know I’m saved?”). Answer simply from Romans 10:9, and tell their parents.',
  tip='Invite, never pressure. If a child prays or asks questions, tell their parents the same day. Celebrate finished maps and give out certificates.',
  care='Children this age are eager to please and influenced by friends. No raised hands, no coming forward, no “every head bowed” counting. Plant the seed and trust God with the timing.',
  ask='Who welcomes us home? Who could our family tell about Jesus?',
  pages_kid=['Put the whole story in order', 'A-B-C and the last verse', 'Gates that open + invite card'], extra=[]),
]
